<GuideLayout
  title="Activewear MOQ Guide: Ready-Stock Trials vs Custom OEM/ODM"
  type="MOQ GUIDE"
  description="Understand Fitclo ready-stock trial quantities and usual OEM/ODM MOQ per style per color. Keep pcs, sets, samples and bulk production terms separate."
>
  <h2>First identify the buying route</h2>
  <p>{businessFacts.readyStock.trial}</p>
  <p>Do not combine separate styles into one minimum without confirmation. A set is one complete coordinated outfit; 50 sets are not 50 individual garments. A logo, custom label or packaging may change the workable route.</p>

  <h2>New OEM/ODM development is a separate order</h2>
  <p>{businessFacts.oem.moq} The quantity is assessed per style and per color, rather than as a total across unrelated SKUs. A stock-style MOQ does not apply to a new pattern, fabric or color.</p>

  <h2>Budget sampling and timing separately</h2>
  <p>{businessFacts.readyStock.sample} {businessFacts.oem.sampleFee}</p>
  <p>{businessFacts.oem.sampleDevelopment} {businessFacts.oem.bulkProduction} {businessFacts.readyStock.dispatch}</p>
  <p>{businessFacts.shipping}</p>

  <h2>What to send for an accurate quote</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Product code or tech pack, and whether you need existing stock or a new design.</li>
    <li>Quantity by style, color and size; state whether each unit is a piece or a complete set.</li>
    <li>Logo, labels, packaging, sample requirements and destination.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
